WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.968
Pedro runs the search for an online auto parts store.

00:00:05.268 --> 00:00:08.918
Vector search did well on brake pads for a compact car.

00:00:09.218 --> 00:00:11.768
It found pads that fit several models.

00:00:12.418 --> 00:00:15.776
Then a customer typed the part number, and nothing else.

00:00:16.076 --> 00:00:20.401
And the search returned pads from other brands, none of them with that code.

00:00:21.051 --> 00:00:24.157
The old keyword search had the opposite problem.

00:00:24.457 --> 00:00:27.682
It got the code right and the natural language wrong.

00:00:27.982 --> 00:00:33.337
Each method fails exactly where the other one succeeds, and customers write both

00:00:33.337 --> 00:00:35.546
ways, often in the same sentence.

00:00:36.196 --> 00:00:41.175
In this lesson, you'll learn to run both searches together, and to combine them so

00:00:41.175 --> 00:00:44.333
one query gets the meaning and the exact term right.

00:00:45.533 --> 00:00:47.131
Think of two librarians.

00:00:47.431 --> 00:00:52.499
One knows the catalog by heart, every code, every acronym, every name.

00:00:52.799 --> 00:00:57.718
The other doesn't remember codes, but understands what you meant, even when you

00:00:57.718 --> 00:00:58.465
asked badly.

00:00:59.115 --> 00:01:01.180
The first is lexical search.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:07.021
It scores documents by the words they contain, and it gets codes, acronyms, names

00:01:07.021 --> 00:01:08.458
and rare words right.

00:01:09.108 --> 00:01:11.032
The second is vector search.

00:01:11.332 --> 00:01:17.176
It scores by closeness in meaning, so synonyms, paraphrases and clumsy questions

00:01:17.176 --> 00:01:17.542
work.

00:01:18.192 --> 00:01:22.737
You ask both, over the same documents, and combine the answers.

00:01:23.037 --> 00:01:25.488
The same trouble shows up outside shops.

00:01:25.788 --> 00:01:30.931
An invoice access key has forty four digits, and only an exact match finds it.

00:01:31.231 --> 00:01:33.754
An embedding approximates meaning.

00:01:34.054 --> 00:01:36.202
It doesn't memorize identifiers.

00:01:37.402 --> 00:01:41.083
The query arrives exactly as the customer wrote it.

00:01:41.733 --> 00:01:46.385
Both searches run in parallel, over the same index, with the same filters.

00:01:46.685 --> 00:01:50.107
Total time is that of the slower one, not the sum.

00:01:50.757 --> 00:01:52.348
Then comes fusion.

00:01:52.648 --> 00:01:56.104
The most common recipe is reciprocal rank fusion.

00:01:56.404 --> 00:02:01.423
Each document earns points for its position in each list, and the sum decides the

00:02:01.423 --> 00:02:02.166
final order.

00:02:02.816 --> 00:02:05.380
It uses positions, not raw scores.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:11.128
And that matters, because keyword scores and vector scores live on different scales,

00:02:11.128 --> 00:02:14.177
and you'd have to calibrate them before adding.

00:02:14.827 --> 00:02:19.900
The top results move on, either as the search results or as the context for a model.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:25.807
Now the mixed sentence: front pad, the part number, for an A4.

00:02:26.107 --> 00:02:31.398
First, the code gets normalized, so the dashes and the case don't matter.

00:02:32.048 --> 00:02:37.307
Keyword search puts the two right products at the top, because they contain the

00:02:37.307 --> 00:02:37.640
code.

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:42.533
Vector search puts at the top pads that fit that car, but without the code.

00:02:43.183 --> 00:02:47.535
One product shows up on both lists, third on one and first on the other.

00:02:47.835 --> 00:02:51.360
It collects points twice, and it rises to first place.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.817
The look-alike pads, without the code, sit lower.

00:02:55.467 --> 00:02:59.261
The mixed sentence stops being a tie between two bad systems.

00:02:59.561 --> 00:03:04.619
Each one contributes what it knows, and fusion promotes whoever showed up on both.

00:03:05.819 --> 00:03:06.799
Think fast.

00:03:07.099 --> 00:03:09.850
A customer types only the part number.

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:13.160
Vector search returns other manufacturers' pads.

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:16.250
Keyword search returns the two right products.

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:20.013
After fusion, the right ones are first and second.

00:03:20.313 --> 00:03:22.976
What did fusion do that was useful?

00:03:26.026 --> 00:03:29.528
It kept at the top what keyword search had already got right.

00:03:29.828 --> 00:03:35.439
When one list is right and the other is wrong, rank fusion preserves the top of the

00:03:35.439 --> 00:03:36.115
good list.

00:03:36.415 --> 00:03:40.163
The vector adds candidates, but it can't bury the code.

00:03:40.813 --> 00:03:43.809
And no, it didn't calibrate any scales.

00:03:44.109 --> 00:03:47.783
The method never looks at raw scores, only at positions.

00:03:48.983 --> 00:03:51.825
Skip it when queries are always identifiers.

00:03:52.125 --> 00:03:57.339
If people only ever paste a code, that's a lookup, and the database already does it

00:03:57.339 --> 00:04:00.982
with an equality check, cheaper and without false matches.

00:04:01.632 --> 00:04:05.854
Skip it when the material is small and fits in the context window.

00:04:06.154 --> 00:04:08.240
Then you don't need search at all.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:14.728
And skip it when vector search already passes the eval, with no failures on exact

00:04:14.728 --> 00:04:15.160
terms.

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:17.405
One search is simpler than two.

00:04:18.055 --> 00:04:19.391
Now the mistakes.

00:04:19.691 --> 00:04:21.991
The classic one is different filters.

00:04:22.291 --> 00:04:27.690
Keyword search respects the category, vector search doesn't, and an out of stock

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:32.886
item from another category leaks into the final list through the vector side.

00:04:33.186 --> 00:04:35.995
The same filter goes into both searches.

00:04:36.645 --> 00:04:39.284
Second, two ids for one product.

00:04:39.584 --> 00:04:44.759
If the text index and the vector index name it differently, fusion never adds the

00:04:44.759 --> 00:04:45.206
points.

00:04:45.856 --> 00:04:49.169
Third, an eval set with only natural sentences.

00:04:49.469 --> 00:04:51.539
You'll conclude that vectors are enough.

00:04:51.839 --> 00:04:56.246
Put codes, invoice keys and internal acronyms in there.

00:04:56.896 --> 00:05:02.196
Also normalize identifiers, before indexing and before searching.

00:05:02.496 --> 00:05:07.898
Code with dashes, code without, code with spaces must become the same token.

00:05:08.198 --> 00:05:12.780
Otherwise keyword search misses the very case it exists for.

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:14.946
What to measure.

00:05:15.246 --> 00:05:17.303
Recall, split by query type.

00:05:17.603 --> 00:05:20.639
Code queries, natural queries, and mixed ones.

00:05:21.289 --> 00:05:23.769
Compare hybrid against each search alone.

00:05:24.069 --> 00:05:29.422
If hybrid doesn't beat keyword search on codes, nor vector search on natural

00:05:29.422 --> 00:05:31.394
sentences, fusion is broken.

00:05:31.694 --> 00:05:36.511
Look at ids, filters and normalization before blaming the domain.

00:05:37.161 --> 00:05:41.460
And log a span with the top of each list and the final list.

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:47.615
On a bad answer, that span tells you whether keyword search, vector search or fusion

00:05:47.615 --> 00:05:48.103
failed.

00:05:48.753 --> 00:05:49.883
One security point.

00:05:50.183 --> 00:05:53.631
Permission filters go into both searches, before fusion.

00:05:53.931 --> 00:05:56.236
Filtering afterwards is late.

00:05:56.536 --> 00:06:01.748
The ranking already mixed in documents the user can't see, and one bug in the post

00:06:01.748 --> 00:06:03.210
filter leaks a passage.

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:06.461
On the page, there are two tabs.

00:06:06.761 --> 00:06:11.789
The Python one runs everything in memory, so you can see fusion with no search

00:06:11.789 --> 00:06:12.240
engine.

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:18.207
It tokenizes with the normalization step, scores with a keyword ranking library, and

00:06:18.207 --> 00:06:20.838
embeds with a small multilingual model.

00:06:21.488 --> 00:06:26.133
Both searches take the same filters and return fifty candidates each.

00:06:26.783 --> 00:06:28.523
Fusion is a few lines.

00:06:28.823 --> 00:06:33.896
Each document gets one over sixty plus its position, from each list, and the points

00:06:33.896 --> 00:06:34.324
add up.

00:06:34.974 --> 00:06:39.533
The TypeScript tab does it inside Postgres, with text search and the vector

00:06:39.533 --> 00:06:40.140
extension.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:46.011
Two queries become two temporary tables, and the same fusion is one SQL statement.

00:06:46.311 --> 00:06:52.195
What you gain is a single database, with identical filters on both searches by

00:06:52.195 --> 00:06:53.176
construction.

00:06:53.476 --> 00:06:57.455
What you give up is fine tuning beyond what Postgres offers.

00:06:58.655 --> 00:07:00.951
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:01.251 --> 00:07:04.705
Meaning without exact terms loses the part number.

00:07:05.005 --> 00:07:09.644
Exact terms without meaning lose the customer who asked badly.

00:07:09.944 --> 00:07:15.123
Run both, over the same documents, with the same filters, and let the ranks vote.

00:07:15.773 --> 00:07:18.017
Hybrid search finds the right result.

00:07:18.317 --> 00:07:20.968
It doesn't always put it where the model will read it.

00:07:21.268 --> 00:07:26.311
If the right passage is at position eighteen and the model only reads five, you need

00:07:26.311 --> 00:07:27.992
a sharper judge for the top.

00:07:28.292 --> 00:07:30.648
That's the next lesson: reranking.

00:07:30.948 --> 00:07:32.317
See you there.
